er-field/id #uuid "117f36b7-d2dd-58cf-81f2-744857f29ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403558}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2d08ab-7e1f-579d-8314-e037ebc00133" :question/math-academy-id "q-7232" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2ad72b3df34f32d6a76f79f6801887bd6fe1d762fefd74071517a754b1890ac2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={2}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={2}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={2}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087427 :question/id #uuid "f73207f4-4661-55f1-acfb-cc5fe6ba9316" :question/math-academy-id "e-1221" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=3^{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1c/1cd695e6f1fab35a692fde9003d452862db33a894c23657eb87f8d773de8cbb9.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c92ea5f3cbfd40bd0dc384b152f6166c27400dbedd93a9c8333195950e7f9c3.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,5)$ to $(0,2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8e8cdccd58a9f1f21c8c2bf0a4a8c08e9c263c4fe91cc92e2bee334de057116.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2c04986-70ee-5052-9ec5-142496777802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c5c95216d03c539a31f8b81b21dd874cb2288c15a5c83b978fd45433f30353a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085351} {:answer/id #uuid "e5acf14e-9406-5c10-aec2-1d303303e0d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/152a5356acdcca5917a01c87b96349f9a587214392bb2e416e11d04e74a3e007.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085352} {:answer/id #uuid "43cd4169-a078-5896-a542-81a9e4dc79bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3ffbfc3a208bb839f2699665ac0e6ffefad9f301158d4c3aafceb828a1f23527.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085353} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae6a940-4764-56e3-afcc-cb11469803e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/1172d95a494114455636188930f09fc1f78e23a4f5820244a5ef777b48c61633.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085354} {:answer/id #uuid "64c969a9-8a2d-5cad-a691-124efd83daf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c428fdf4f66ce7c80d64daa714af81befb2908e1da1aac5580425ae2425730c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085355}] :answer-field/id #uuid "872ff259-84a8-5c41-8343-8fd5a0f06a93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085350}] :question/id #uuid "2d06cd58-841e-5527-a294-4dae48a373bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-26910" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y = 4(6^{x}) - 2$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0718a36d-396f-5368-85ba-5007af7edbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295300} {:answer/id #uuid "102df500-4571-5550-8394-ccf1449c0d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295361} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7e6108-fabc-5a0c-af99-2e167084609b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295621} {:answer/id #uuid "4ace8761-2cf1-5067-a981-dd24a6032050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295692} {:answer/id #uuid "9774f025-3ad5-5dd7-8c8e-12ea3987a542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ace8761-2cf1-5067-a981-dd24a6032050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295692} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f42c0d6-5043-56d2-b372-7673a9d172a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296830}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9e76cd0-6e63-52b6-ad92-c0e43ec54487" :question/math-academy-id "q-30270" :question/problem "For the curve $y=5({6}^{x})+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={6}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=5({6}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=5+2=7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5({6}^{0})+2 \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =5+2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=5({6}^{x})+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b5bc36-e007-593f-8d2c-a16b08fc3d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281912} {:answer/id #uuid "60bb1b4f-6b88-526c-9dab-0b8304b3f942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282510} {:answer/id #uuid "99796e47-234a-5f62-9f0a-956c0cd56a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283024} {:answer/id #uuid "99e0b67c-9be5-5fe2-ba59-a19376e720ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283028} {:answer/id #uuid "aa64d2f2-0944-5be4-be52-ad85329db1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99e0b67c-9be5-5fe2-ba59-a19376e720ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283028} :answer-field/id #uuid "a670f1f8-5c9f-5514-826c-a3d0cc3426e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59cc81c5-fc9d-517e-99cf-c3bfb1a3b17a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170594" :question/problem "For the curve $y=1.5({4}^{x})+1,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={4}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({4}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $1$ unit up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5+1=2.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({4}^{0})+1 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1+1 \\\\  & =1.5+1 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({4}^{x})+1,$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2.5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0265ab4b-e69c-58cd-bb39-877b73f3298f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,11.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295275} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab493a4-b37f-5582-a8b9-4570920c001f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295861} {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdd579a-68d8-530f-aec2-948295be5064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296522} {:answer/id #uuid "f541b6c1-d1ab-5c71-9e34-c29b7b48861a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0972749-6df3-5836-b156-61b2f017bcaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91d38446-45fe-5f55-b13c-726fa96fa8c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-30269" :question/problem "For the curve $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={1.5}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=2.5({1.5}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2.5\\cdot 1=2.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2.5+8=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.5({1.5}^{0})+8 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 1+8 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,10.5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e5493f0-49e5-5ac0-a64b-444f26facf5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295811} {:answer/id #uuid "cedf3ffd-b468-57c5-a1fb-5644942d8367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296352} {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e90daa-a470-5b92-aeb7-f90fc7f25a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296492} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3169b4-14de-5f3c-837e-5d327959c0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef5eb88-b39d-569b-bc4c-949ed152d723" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296969}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fda2bca-a959-55aa-9acd-07aede112347" :question/math-academy-id "q-30873" :question/problem "For the curve $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={10}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({10}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8.5$ units down, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5-8.5=-7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({10}^{0})-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5-8.5 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16cf5275-48a4-5d52-bfe0-a5c956f020ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eed5f150b826a708834eb9d7c4595cbdcc0113842fc0b172c1cf11e65681b199.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336246} {:answer/id #uuid "1accd3cf-7928-5942-9ce7-0ac1d86bacc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5c68724af1732ee023599ce2969c165dfc68b8e198e14cebbc1262bbf1bd881.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336280} {:answer/id #uuid "4282c634-a61a-54b3-9818-ffa50eb3be52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/75/752c0102b1c019f5bc13673b0107627bf58954fb5ef5b80d3b43e33e5f46f55d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336661} {:answer/id #uuid "8aed9c65-0e6d-56b4-abe0-e8d553ce98e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/960ebb31bb1330c7def20222f012e2c89d0f2f4f588cabb914c42c60571dfcdc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337286} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4ecd2b-a163-51b1-add4-86835f9e1935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/580a0d2a241a867159827d1570d20bb120e70297fef4b68c103fc580bbb2b224.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aed9c65-0e6d-56b4-abe0-e8d553ce98e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/960ebb31bb1330c7def20222f012e2c89d0f2f4f588cabb914c42c60571dfcdc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337286} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fd8ca31-a85e-5970-a1f2-11e22bec8157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338366}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3548d09b-2351-5856-9855-c3a656fa374f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7121" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2({3}^{x})-4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={3}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/1c/1cd695e6f1fab35a692fde9003d452862db33a894c23657eb87f8d773de8cbb9.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=2({3}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/5a/5a9871ac900e85c9f47118f6233b2e7f094af010dcb73e54221cadac01ce1644.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=2({3}^{x})-4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](images/89/89a7ee023e8d4232b617052efdc9844cb00b2e3f05968119cea11c89242ca61a.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087428 :question/id #uuid "62f7d6e1-61ec-5f3d-8cdd-4da0c90c7ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-1222" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k(6^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](images/de/deb7e20658882229a3653f4058c787bf2a5c4a26c5f2982406a2dba34c2d5846.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k(6^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4$, so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({6}^{x})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k({6}^{0})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 6 & =k+4 \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2(6^{x})+4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725 :topic/math-academy-id 65 :topic/title "Solving One-Step Addition and Subtraction Equations"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdb66394-4266-565e-91ae-8a8b7af9cee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k = 2$ and $c = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085101} {:answer/id #uuid "150b9369-075a-5021-bc4c-61c7a18b4f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k = 2$ and $c =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085102} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbf6db9-8223-5028-ae5e-f807b2dd3498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k = 2$ and $c =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085103} {:answer/id #uuid "699bb0a1-016d-57fe-92f5-0428a434dc65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k = 0$ and $c =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085104} {:answer/id #uuid "6a125372-2aec-5d48-8233-b14a97167e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k = 2$ and $c = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085105}] :answer-field/id #uuid "74bd184c-dd38-5a0d-bfa3-9b28f2449073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085100}] :question/id #uuid "0f849808-e14b-5ed1-9f45-9decbc5d48b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-18518" :question/problem "![](images/67/67f035c4d578afbbf7de0daa17c2796c49dd194b32daa6a5d36b0dcefc610ea2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y = k(2^{x}) + c$. Find the value of $k$ and $c$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c922058f-6ffc-5196-8a63-c6d648cf5eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2(2^{x}) - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085107} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ee1b66-a62b-52df-92bc-6bade2c00445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3(2^{x}) - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085108} {:answer/id #uuid "e36ec578-8a82-5f24-8d5e-4cf41bf3a472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2(3^{x}) - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085109} {:answer/id #uuid "0c025b92-f6e1-52f1-b4cc-dd437db7b61b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3(3^{x}) - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085110} {:answer/id #uuid "44ba58e9-8529-5e68-b6b0-7622e301e7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3(3^{x}) - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085111}] :answer-field/id #uuid "25d38c7d-33d0-53a4-bcae-e71f013b8dda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085106}] :question/id #uuid "a6888b0a-da93-558b-a1d6-b461e8143a48" :question/math-academy-id "q-18521" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe213ea54dfd14c37ae9674bd025fcd57b0ca7fe3db12f1885cb91fac2d2bf1f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y = k(3^{x}) + c$. Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3033adc0-2183-5e2c-b2ab-63195c598803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=0$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295542} {:answer/id #uuid "474dd8bc-1c5d-55ec-8e0e-e9ca0f43cf6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-2$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295674} {:answer/id #uuid "9c47edb1-a830-59d9-96ec-8a3e4d43e2c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=0$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296101} {:answer/id #uuid "a8da0063-cf36-5ff9-9624-61c1f1ff3246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=2$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296155} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bae8ee-b0c7-5e6e-a0d2-0ebf31e953d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=3$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bae8ee-b0c7-5e6e-a0d2-0ebf31e953d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=3$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296211} :answer-field/id #uuid "523ef40f-5529-5bc7-bc94-9155612cb37b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296799}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f23367e0-950a-5c59-9df0-bbc47cc55447" :question/math-academy-id "q-29233" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e20c8b73d3e46aa90c5d37a30d8343c98d6c6b0f3f17a232580583a8ae812125.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$ Find the value of $k$ and $c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ so $c=-2.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-2.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k({2}^{0})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-2 \\\\ 1 & =k-2 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=3$ and $c=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121b16a2-0458-5600-9a6f-f4f6e552fd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336202} {:answer/id #uuid "4c671da3-c302-5f3c-9ea5-2dc6dac2cc6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336742} {:answer/id #uuid "6fed05ea-acde-5be8-b414-01135dc6f042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337023} {:answer/id #uuid "826ab4ce-caa8-5db7-9188-f1b3cd74a9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337218} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf95245-48b9-524a-917b-0ba71da5cdb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fed05ea-acde-5be8-b414-01135dc6f042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337023} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eab9c10-198f-58c5-918e-000cc777273e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338303}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f0333a8-0cc0-5b0c-9999-f5175286f364" :question/math-academy-id "q-7239" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1ca11413bbce2ff307e494fb5a79028535b76cff24619e6921620f88c3e274ec.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1,$ so $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-1.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k({2}^{0})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k(1)-1 \\\\ 2 & =k-1 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3({2}^{x})-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49005b9e-8885-518c-a503-38932dbc77f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336710} {:answer/id #uuid "a7777608-b4d4-5368-84cb-c7a407165b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337546} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7873cf-81b9-5aaf-8051-9f548d554ab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337684} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad9d29-36d5-5b4e-bad4-d6181862fae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337768} {:answer/id #uuid "e97102d4-c948-5475-a8f4-3282b09cc04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0.5({2}^{x})+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49005b9e-8885-518c-a503-38932dbc77f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336710} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a06ece-0b13-5c0a-9459-301060485f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338290}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a92c523-6f37-5b9d-8ed7-2c5b4d7360ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-7241" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a78c4fc61bbf60e1cdfdba6303ecc1a9d5e09caa21d0879556106893a931a09.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,5.5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=5.5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k({2}^{0})+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k+4 \\\\ 1.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=1.5({2}^{x})+4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ce58e5a-cdfc-5db9-b925-fbed74ce33e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({3}^{x})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336872} {:answer/id #uuid "6965cfa3-9de6-50d2-97f1-3e9a39053046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({3}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336969} {:answer/id #uuid "9190d101-9cc9-5b59-9ff1-c543b6d33077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({3}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337349} {:answer/id #uuid "e78f0489-7a6c-5a4a-b1ab-06362d7a58cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({3}^{x})+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338056} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3098c0-1ba2-5d6f-b48b-c270f7d26cf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({3}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9190d101-9cc9-5b59-9ff1-c543b6d33077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({3}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337349} :answer-field/id #uuid "374f5b58-fd1b-55d0-8e9a-19ddbb2f2f30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338376}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0675fd56-37a7-52dc-92c0-56b23003d598" :question/math-academy-id "q-7243" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff1a81920ab2c3f7870479be47fc46f223e148d32ee01b5e172a4fadb7a98a50.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({3}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({3}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2,$ so $c=2.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({3}^{x})+2.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({3}^{x})+2 \\\\ 5 & =k({3}^{0})+2 \\\\ 5 & =k(1)+2 \\\\ 5 & =k+2 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3({3}^{x})+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab10718-6d3b-52c0-979d-851a802bf503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({5}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360942} {:answer/id #uuid "1de876ec-9970-58f2-9a90-c4d11e4cb17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({5}^{x})-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360960} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9bf016-9043-5261-918f-1b6e78a04dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({5}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361123} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c6d0f2-103a-5ff1-ae93-ddea26192443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4({5}^{x})-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361626} {:answer/id #uuid "dd530816-df7a-59d6-bf4f-d28add78a64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({5}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1de876ec-9970-58f2-9a90-c4d11e4cb17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({5}^{x})-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360960} :answer-field/id #uuid "91df8c96-23a0-5454-9a78-60cda5ffe269" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362114}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4aab5f63-2056-5d00-a333-f4b526199883" :question/math-academy-id "q-99778" :question/problem "![](images/51/5119e42566eff8078bdcf38d8c521e291f902bf1f03f6ddb00321989e3c94c5a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({5}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({5}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ so $c=-2.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({5}^{x})-2.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({5}^{x})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k({5}^{0})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-2 \\\\ 1 & =k-2 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3({5}^{x})-2.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}]}
